  "Number": 32,
  "Question": "If \\(\\triangle ABC\\) is inscribed in a semicircle whose diameter is \\(\\overline{AB},\\) then \\(\\overline{AC}+\\overline{BC}\\) must be:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Equal to \\(\\overline{AB}\\)",
    "B": "Equal to \\(\\overline{AB}\\sqrt2\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\ge \\overline{AB}\\sqrt2\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\le \\overline{AB}\\sqrt2\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\overline{AB}^{\\,2}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "A diameter subtends a right angle at any other point on its semicircle, so \\(\\angle ACB=90^\\circ\\). Let \\(u=AC\\), \\(v=BC\\), and \\(d=AB\\). These lengths are positive, and the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\\[\nu^2+v^2=d^2.\n\\]\nTo bound the sum of the legs, use the fact that a square cannot be negative:\n\\[\n(u+v)^2=2(u^2+v^2)-(u-v)^2\\le2d^2.\n\\]\nBoth \\(u+v\\) and \\(d\\) are positive, so taking nonnegative square roots gives\n\\[\nAC+BC\\le AB\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nEquality holds exactly when \\(u=v\\), or \\(AC=BC\\). Thus the relation that must hold is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
